detail.abstract:
This is the response I delivered to the panel on International Tax Cooperation After Sevilla – From Commitments to Action, convened by the President of the ECOSOC, during the Special Meeting on International Cooperation in Tax Matters on 27 March 2026.&nbsp;
<div>
 It highlights three points:&nbsp; 
 <div>
  1) on taxation and sustainability, arguing that the relationship between the tax system and environmental and social harm extends well beyond consumer-facing levies, requiring reforming profit allocation rules that are indifferent to where harm takes place and corporate tax expenditures that effectively subsidize unsustainable behavior;&nbsp;
 </div>
 <div>
  2) on wealth taxation and progressivity, arguing that progress requires coordinated legal design beyond information exchange, given the structural and legal obstacles posed by mobile and hard-to-value assets; and&nbsp;
 </div>
 <div>
  3) on corporate taxation and global redistribution, arguing that domestic minimum source-based taxation functions as a global wealth-sharing mechanism that secures for host countries a share of the cooperative surplus generated by cross-border economic activity.
 </div>
</div>
